<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<sdf version='1.9'>
  <!--
    3-tendon soft continuum arm: Ø40mm OD / Ø30mm ID lattice-TPU tube
    (behaves like a traditional silicone continuum arm) - same physical arm
    as continuum_arm_cosserat, modeled here with a piecewise-constant-
    curvature (PCC) kinematic approximation instead of the full Cosserat
    rod solve. Discretized as N=15 rigid segments (total length L=0.40m,
    ~26.67mm per segment) coupled by 2-DOF universal joints (axis=X,
    axis2=Y bending). Segment visual/collision geometry stays a solid
    outer-radius (20mm) cylinder for simplicity (SDF has no annulus
    primitive); the <inertial> mass/inertia below are computed from the TRUE
    hollow-tube cross-section instead - see gen_pcc_arm.py.

    Passive elastic compliance is modeled with native joint spring dynamics
    on every axis (K_theta = E*I/l_s = 1.6107 N*m/rad, damping 0.08),
    where E, I are the SAME Young's modulus and true hollow-tube second
    moment of area continuum_arm_cosserat's solver uses for its Kbt - i.e.
    this joint spring constant isn't an independently-tuned number, it's the
    lumped-parameter (one torsional spring per segment) equivalent of the
    same distributed-elasticity beam model, so both arm models represent
    physically consistent material behavior despite the different
    mechanics formulations (kinematic split vs. full BVP solve).

    Active bending comes from TendonArmController, which converts a
    commanded 3-tendon length vector (l1,l2,l3) into constant-curvature
    target angles (theta_x, theta_y) via the standard 3-tendon Jacobian
    (pitch radius = tendon routing radius = 15mm = ID/2, matching the 3
    tendons run along the inner wall's 30mm-diameter circle, 120deg apart)
    and drives every joint axis toward theta_x/N, theta_y/N with a PD force
    loop layered on top of the passive spring (see
    plugins/tendon_arm_controller).

    Each segment link's own origin is at its geometric center; joints are
    offset (child-relative pose, +half segment length along local Z) up to
    the proximal/top face of that segment, where it welds to the previous
    segment's (or the base cylinder's) bottom face. Arm hangs along -Z from
    the base cylinder's bottom face, matching a bottom-mounted attachment.

    Mass budget: total assembly (base + 15 segments) is ~250g, split as
    m_base=78.43g + 15*m_seg=11.44g = 250.00g, using the
    same uniform effective density (780.2 kg/m^3, lattice-infill TPU) as
    continuum_arm_cosserat - see gen_pcc_arm.py.

    Mount contract: continuum_arm_pcc/base_cyl is this module's own
    attachment point, its top face (z=0 in this file's frame) is the
    mounting interface. Composing this onto a vehicle (e.g. hillstar) needs
    one glue joint (parent=<vehicle base link>, child=
    continuum_arm_pcc/base_cyl), the same pattern rigid_arm_3link and
    hillstar_rigid_arm use - see that model's header comment for why the
    joint must be declared in the composing file rather than in here.
  -->
  <model name='continuum_arm_pcc'>
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>
    <static>false</static>

    <link name="continuum_arm_pcc/base_cyl">
      <pose>0 0 -0.01000 0 0 0</pose>
      <gravity>true</gravity>
      <self_collide>false</self_collide>
      <velocity_decay/>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.078431</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>3.398693e-05</ixx>
          <iyy>3.398693e-05</iyy>
          <izz>6.274510e-05</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <collision name="continuum_arm_pcc/base_cyl_collision">
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.04</radius><length>0.02</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <visual name="continuum_arm_pcc/base_cyl_visual">
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.04</radius><length>0.02</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.05 0.08 0.20 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.08 0.12 0.30 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <!-- Segment 1/15 -->
    <link name="continuum_arm_pcc/segment_1">
      <pose>0 0 -0.03333 0 0 0</pose>
      <gravity>true</gravity>
      <self_collide>false</self_collide>
      <velocity_decay/>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.011438</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>2.464975e-06</ixx>
          <iyy>2.464975e-06</iyy>
          <izz>3.574346e-06</izz>
